## Date-time construction

- Create `TimePoint` values from structured arguments
- Missing fields are filled as lower-bound defaults
  - year → January 1st, 00:00:00
  - month → day 1, 00:00:00
  - day → 00:00:00
  - hour → minute 0, second 0
  - minute → second 0
- Precision is inferred from provided arguments
- Full validation of date and time components

## String parsing

- Parse string input into `TimePoint`
- Supports variable precision (year → millisecond)
- Rejects invalid formats and values

## Current time

- Retrieve current UTC time (`TimePoint::now_utc`)
- Always returns millisecond-level precision

## Precision model

- Explicit precision levels:
  - Year
  - Month
  - Day
  - Hour
  - Minute
  - Second
  - Millisecond
- Precision determines semantic meaning and interval size

## Interval normalization

- Convert `TimePoint` to `Interval`
- Represent time as half-open interval `[lower, upper)`
- Automatically compute upper bound based on precision

## Temporal comparison

- All comparisons are interval-based
- Supported operations:
  - before
  - after
  - equals
  - contains
  - overlaps
- Results are three-valued (`True` / `False` / `Unknown`):
  - Exact (`True` / `False`) when the intervals are certainly ordered, equal, or disjoint
  - `Unknown` when the intervals could overlap

## Duration

- `Duration` is a fixed elapsed amount of time with millisecond resolution
- No calendar concepts: months and years are not representable (see the future `Period`)
- Signed: positive, negative, and zero durations
- Construction: `from_milliseconds`, `from_seconds`, `from_minutes`, `from_hours`, `zero`
- Accessors: `milliseconds`, `seconds`, `subsec_milliseconds`, `is_zero`, `is_negative`, `is_positive`
- Operators: `+`, `-`, unary `-`, equality and ordering
- TimePoint arithmetic:
  - `TimePoint + Duration -> TimePoint`
  - `TimePoint - Duration -> TimePoint`
  - `TimePoint - TimePoint -> Duration`
- Arithmetic is calendar-aware and leap-second-aware (a leap second counts as one elapsed second)
- Millisecond precision is preserved; `TimePoint` precision metadata is unchanged

## Period

- `Period` is a calendar-relative amount of time: years, months, days, plus a fixed time part (hours, minutes, seconds, milliseconds)
- Unlike `Duration` (fixed elapsed time), months and years vary in length; a `Period` only becomes concrete once anchored to a `TimePoint`
- Construction: `new`, `from_years`, `from_months`, `from_days`, `from_hours`, `from_minutes`, `from_seconds`, `from_milliseconds`, `zero`
- Accessors for each component, `is_zero`, `is_negative`
- Operators: `+`, `-`, unary `-` (components combine and normalize: 12 months → 1 year)
- TimePoint arithmetic:
  - `TimePoint + Period -> TimePoint`
  - `TimePoint - Period -> TimePoint`
- Calendar semantics:
  - Adding years/months clamps the day to the last valid day of the target month (`2024-02-29 + 1 year` → `2025-02-28`, `2027-01-31 + 1 month` → `2027-02-28`)
  - The date part applies first (years → months → days), then the fixed time part (leap-second-aware)
  - The time of day is preserved across day arithmetic
- Display uses ISO 8601-style notation (`P1Y2M3DT4H5M6S`, `-P1Y`)

## Uncertainty model

- Attach a symmetric offset to any `TimePoint` via `with_uncertainty(Uncertainty::from_seconds(n))`
- Default is no uncertainty (`uncertainty: None`)
- An uncertain point `t ± u` denotes the semantic interval `[t-u, t+u)`
- Uncertainty takes precedence over precision when deriving intervals
- Relations between uncertain points widen both intervals; the three-valued result reports
  - `True` when the relation certainly holds
  - `False` when it certainly does not
  - `Unknown` when the widened intervals could still overlap
- Example: `12:00:00 ± 10s` is `[11:59:50, 12:00:10)`; with `12:10:00 ± 10s` the relation
  `before` is certainly `True`, while `12:00:00 ± 10min` vs `12:05:00 ± 10min` is `Unknown`
- Arithmetic propagates uncertainty: `(t ± u) + d = (t + d) ± u` for seconds, durations, and periods
  (for periods, this is the standard shift-the-center approximation when calendar clamping applies)
- The low-level `add_one_*`/`sub_one_*` boundary helpers still return points without uncertainty

## Ordering

- Implements `Ord` and `PartialOrd`
- Lexicographic comparison including time components

## Encoding

- Encode date into compact integer representation
- Encode full datetime into ordered `u64`
- Encodings preserve chronological ordering, including across second/minute/hour/day/month/year boundaries and leap seconds

## Rollover handling

- Correct transitions:
  - second → minute
  - minute → hour
  - hour → day
  - day → month
  - month → year

## Leap year support

- Validates February 29 correctly
- Rejects invalid leap dates

## Leap second support

- Fetch leap second data (or load local `leap-seconds.list`)
- Identify leap second days where `23:59:60` is valid
- Correct rollover across leap seconds in arithmetic

## Unix interoperability

- Convert `TimePoint` to/from Unix timestamps
- Unix cannot represent leap seconds: second 60 is rejected

## TAI interoperability

- Convert UTC ↔ TAI
- TAI-UTC offset calculation from the leap-second table
- Leap seconds are absorbed by the offset in TAI (no second 60)
- TAI seconds falling during a leap second map back to UTC `23:59:60`

## Time zones

- `TimeZone::UTC`, `TimeZone::TAI`, `TimeZone::Unix`, and fixed-offset zones
- `TimeZone::fixed(hours, minutes)` builds a fixed-offset zone such as `-05:00` or `+05:30` (hours -14..=14, minutes 0..=59)
- A `TimePoint`'s fields are its local wall clock in its zone
- `with_zone` attaches a zone without converting; `convert_to` / `to_utc` convert between zones, preserving precision and uncertainty
- Fixed-offset zones keep their offset constant (no DST transitions); leap seconds are UTC-anchored
- `to_unix_timestamp` accounts for the zone offset; TAI and leap-second points are rejected

## Module layout

- `time_point.rs` - temporal value with precision, time zone, and uncertainty
- `duration.rs` - fixed elapsed time with millisecond resolution
- `period.rs` - calendar-relative amount (years, months, days, time)
- `precision.rs` - precision levels
- `interval.rs` - half-open `[start, end)` intervals and three-valued relations
- `time_zone.rs` - UTC, TAI, Unix, fixed-offset zones
- `truth_values.rs` - True, False, Unknown
- `uncertainty.rs` - symmetric uncertainty offset in seconds
- `leap_second.rs` - leap-second data handling
- `util.rs` - calendar validation and helpers
- `unix.rs` - Unix timestamp conversion
- `tai.rs` - TAI conversion and offset calculation

Integration tests live in `tests/`.